00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.280
So APIs are powering innovation.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.180
And just to start with a little
bit of the history there,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.320
APIs have been around
for about 20 years

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.240
and they were created
out of a need.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.640
In the early 2000s,
APIs were first

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.823
used by companies
like Amazon and eBay,

00:01:28.823 --> 00:01:30.990
who were noticing unofficial
usage of their services

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.128
and data.

00:01:32.128 --> 00:01:34.170
These companies needed a
way to provide structure

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.510
to their developers, their
partners, and their customers

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.270
in a secure way
and scalable way.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.648
And now, APIs are
everywhere and they're

00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:43.440
a key enabler to
innovation because they're

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.630
facilitating new ways for
customers and partners

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.300
to interact with each other.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.130
They're also enabling
new use cases

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.010
by connecting legacy
infrastructure to modern web

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.070
apps and mobile apps.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.690
APIs can also enable use cases.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.660
So there's voice
ordering from a phone

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.910
to connecting brick
and mortar companies,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.760
moving them into an
e-commerce future,

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.070
and connecting
partners and services.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.570
So an example here about--

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.800
I'll talk about
Walgreens for a minute.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.210
They have thousands of locations
and they're leveraging-- just

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.800
to mention a couple
of their APIs--

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.720
they have a store locator API.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.650
And Fitbit is actually using
that to enable Fitbit customers

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.130
to easily locate a
Walgreens location

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.010
and use Fitbit Pay to
actually pay in the stores.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.350
So there's a couple of
different APIs being used there.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.680
They also have a
photo printing API,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.480
and this enables
third-party apps

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.240
to print to a Walgreens
location and pick up

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.240
photos in the store.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.860
So this is driving customers
and traffic to a retail location

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.580
and enabling third-party
app developers

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.420
to have a rev share
model with Walgreens.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.800
So it's a good win-win
situation there,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.360
but driving more
customers to the stores.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.920
If we take a look
at Magazine Luiza--

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.560
this is one of Brazil's
largest retailers--

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.600
they've been around
for more than 60 years.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.260
They realize that it's not only
about physical brick and mortar

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:07.980
anymore.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.560
They wanted to move into
the e-commerce space,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.830
and APIs have enabled them to
scale to thousands of delivery

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.570
partners and increase
their product

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.480
catalog going from
50,000 products

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.830
to 1.5 million products.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.710
So they had a 30x increase
in the number of products

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:27.420
by enabling 2,000 merchants to
support and sell their products

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.470
on their e-commerce platform.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.370
As you can see here,
the financial impact

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.120
has been incredible.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.320
So we're seeing trends from
customers and in many cases,

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:40.840
security is an afterthought.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.030
It's how do we design the--

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.050
what are the problems
we want to solve,

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.918
what's the customer
requirements,

00:03:46.918 --> 00:03:47.960
and how do we build that?

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.970
And security is one more
thing they need to deal with.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.150
And in many cases, it's
an afterthought and that

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.400
puts user and data at risk.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.090
So a lot of the data
that's exposed today,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.150
we're talking payment
information, PII, PHI banking

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.580
information.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.000
If you don't have the proper
visibility into what is exposed

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.130
and how it's exposed,
you're creating

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:12.750
a lot of risk and liability.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.860
And this is why having a
full lifecycle API management

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.380
solution is really
needed to enable

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.570
the way-- a secure
way of enabling

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.420
the exposure of
this information.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.730
So just to give a
high-level overview of what

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:30.230
Apigee's offering, Apigee is an
API management platform and it

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:31.030
offers--

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.900
breaks into three
components here.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.690
From a developer/management
perspective,

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:38.360
we're providing a secure
way for developers

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.710
to scale their APIs
to a larger audience

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.442
and consume the information
in a managed way.

00:04:43.442 --> 00:04:44.900
We're offering API
analytics, which

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.580
gives you clear
insights to measure,

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.470
tune, and monitor
your API program.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.860
And I'll dive in a little bit
more about the API monitoring

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.090
in a few minutes.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.940
And API services give
you complete control

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.370
by allowing you to manage the
connection between the API

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.680
consumer and the back end
infrastructure and service,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.082
so you can have complete
control over the request

00:05:04.082 --> 00:05:04.790
and the response.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.420
So just to give an
example from Google Maps,

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.640
if you search for a
hotel for this week,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.510
you can now choose dates that
you want to check into a room.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.700
You see the availability
of that room,

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.910
and you can actually get live
pricing data in Google Maps

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:28.040
because this is being powered by
APIs from these third parties.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.650
And this now allows
you to book directly

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:34.383
in the mobile app
or the web app,

00:05:34.383 --> 00:05:36.050
or through thousands
of travel partners.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.540
So Zen Hotels, Kayak,
all of these guys

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.520
have a relationship
with the St. Regis.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.270
So this is just a quick
example of how powerful APIs

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:47.193
can be as opposed to the old
world of manually updating

00:05:47.193 --> 00:05:48.860
pricing across different
sites, and when

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.540
something is sold
out, the time it

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.940
takes to remove those listings.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:57.440
So this has been very powerful
in Google Maps and Search.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.620
But discussing Walgreen's,
Magazine Luiza,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.630
and Google Maps, these
are a few examples

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:05.780
where APIs have been
powering business innovation

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.120
and disruption in
different markets.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.990
But in some cases, disruption
is not always a good thing.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.720
And when security's
an afterthought,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.970
sometimes enterprises don't
take the right precautions

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.958
before exposing their
APIs and services.

00:06:19.958 --> 00:06:21.500
And in many cases,
it's simply a lack

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.200
of awareness of what's exposed,
what controls are in place,

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.890
and what is actually
happening with the data.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.135
So just to talk a
minute about the USPS,

00:06:31.135 --> 00:06:32.885
and there's no shortage
of these examples,

00:06:32.885 --> 00:06:36.830
so I'm just going to
highlight this one.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.810
They offer a new service
for their customers

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:40.340
called Informed
Visibility, and this

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:45.500
allows businesses and users
to gain the ability to track--

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.420
near real-time tracking
for mail campaigns.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.470
So the issue they had really
breaks into two parts.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:55.647
They misconfigured their API
and allowed wild card input.

00:06:55.647 --> 00:06:57.980
Joel will talk a little bit
more about input validation,

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.663
but that was not the
right way to do it.

00:07:00.663 --> 00:07:03.080
And they also had no visibility
into what data was exposed

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:05.000
or who's accessing that data.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.490
So the configuration
that they deployed

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.290
allowed any user
to view or change

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.510
any other user's information,
such as the email address,

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.690
phone number, and other PII.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:19.520
And this is far more harmful
than a single user's account

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:20.603
being compromised.

00:07:20.603 --> 00:07:22.520
If someone hacks into a
single user's account,

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.562
they have all of their
data, and that's obviously

00:07:24.562 --> 00:07:25.380
not a good thing.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:27.470
But, in this case, 60
million users' data

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.050
was exposed for several months.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.330
And this could have
been avoided if they

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:35.210
used proper [INAUDIBLE] scopes
and simply didn't allow access

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:36.553
to other users' data.

00:07:36.553 --> 00:07:38.720
Or, as Joel will talk about
later, input validation,

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:40.845
where they simply should
not have allowed wild card

00:07:40.845 --> 00:07:45.080
input to prevent multiple
records from being extracted.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:49.010
And you won't always be able to
prevent every misconfiguration.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:50.300
People are human.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.520
But there are a lot of
processes and methods

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.433
you can take in an
approach to ensure

00:07:54.433 --> 00:07:56.100
that these kinds of
things don't happen.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.843
And again, Joel's going to talk
a little bit more about that.

00:07:58.843 --> 00:08:00.260
But this is another
example of why

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.600
having visibility into
who's accessing your data

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:05.920
is extremely important.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.310
So it's just
happening too often.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.717
So there's an article
from "Dark Reading."

00:08:11.717 --> 00:08:13.300
In the first nine
months of last year,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.277
there were 3,600
reported breaches.

00:08:15.277 --> 00:08:17.110
So this isn't just about
the postal service.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:20.080
And the fourth-largest data
breach of the 21st century

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.660
was due to an API vulnerability.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:24.840
Gartner's estimating by 2022--

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.160
so this is only a
couple of years out--

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.080
API abuse will be the
most frequent attack

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.320
vector resulting in data
breaches for enterprise web

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:33.440
applications.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.850
So with thousands of
breaches last year--

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:39.825
and keep in mind, outside
of the EU, with GDPR,

00:08:39.825 --> 00:08:41.950
they're actually required
to report these breaches.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:44.300
But in most of the
rest of the world,

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.660
they're not actually required
to report these on their own.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:52.160
So the number is likely an order
of magnitude higher than this.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.832
So it really boils
down to the fact

00:08:53.832 --> 00:08:55.540
that you can't secure
what you can't see.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.643
So let's just take a quick poll.

00:08:57.643 --> 00:08:58.310
Raise your hand.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.150
How many people are using APIs
in their environment today?

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:02.360
OK, everybody.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.100
So keep your hands up.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.840
How many are using an
API management platform?

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.130
About half of those.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.980
And how many feel like they
have the proper visibility

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.780
into their APIs?

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:16.550
Three, four, OK.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:20.450
So we did a poll on
this a couple weeks ago

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.580
and an overwhelming majority
felt like they did not

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.020
have the proper visibility.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:27.140
And having visibility
into what risk exists

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.870
and what has changed
is the first step.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:32.690
And generally, how well your
service is performing and what

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:34.670
threats exist-- what
insider threats exists,

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:36.840
what anomalies are you
seeing in your traffic--

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.010
these are all critical aspects
to a successful API program.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.320
And every API doesn't
face the same challenges,

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.370
but having that visibility into
rates of attacks, and latency

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:49.490
of responses, understanding how
your back end infrastructure's

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:52.045
architected to better educate
teams on how to respond

00:09:52.045 --> 00:09:53.420
will put you in
a better position

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:57.030
if something does
happen in the future.

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.690
So Apigee is taking a
two-pronged approach

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.040
to visibility.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.650
We're offering insights to meet
the needs of both operations

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.220
and security teams.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:08.660
So today users are
expecting 100% availability.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:09.740
We talk about five nines.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.650
The expectation is 100%.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.230
And Google research
from a couple of years

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:18.698
ago, more than 53% of users
will close a mobile app

00:10:18.698 --> 00:10:20.990
or leave a web page if it
takes more than three seconds

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:25.100
to load, so the
bar is quite high.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:28.190
And you need to be
able to identify issues

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:29.340
before your customers do.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.300
And as you can see, Apigee
is offering clear insights

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.390
into where requests
are coming from,

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:36.530
what latency exists by region.

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:38.360
You can generate custom reports.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.700
And it's always better
to be alerted of issues

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:42.200
before your customers alert you.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.315
So you don't want to learn
about something saying,

00:10:44.315 --> 00:10:46.190
hey, why did the app
installs drop this week,

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:49.040
or web traffic drop, or why is
support having more customer

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:49.910
escalations?

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:51.560
You want to be
notified before that.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.720
And for many of our
customers, security issues

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:56.910
are initially detected
as operational issues.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:59.580
So maybe a large spike in
traffic is a good thing.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.310
Maybe tickets for an
event went on sale,

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:04.200
or it's a flash sale
or something like that.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:07.100
But if it isn't, this is
likely of concern and something

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.870
you should know about.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.850
Apigee gives you the
ability to take action

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:14.840
based on contextual alerts
before your customers are

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:15.420
impacted.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:19.610
So by having access to this
data and leveraging Apigee fault

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.700
codes, you can easily
isolate problems and speed up

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.950
the diagnosis quickly.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.820
So from an API security
reporting perspective,

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.850
Apigee has all of the data
that security admins need

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:35.922
across various parts
of our platform.

00:11:35.922 --> 00:11:37.630
And we've been seeing
an increasing trend

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.450
of collaboration between
ops and security teams

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:43.430
working together to investigate
and track down issues.

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:47.017
So we wanted to make this
information easily accessible

00:11:47.017 --> 00:11:48.850
with an at-a-glance
view, so you can quickly

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.410
know if there's something
to be concerned about.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.150
And we announced
this on Tuesday,

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.020
but we are excited that we are
now offering the API security

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.140
reporting in both a
dashboard and an API,

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:01.780
so you can easily integrate with
existing [? Sim ?] tools that

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.322
you're using today-- existing
processes that you already have

00:12:04.322 --> 00:12:04.840
in place--

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:10.120
or in the Apigee Edge console
as a security dashboard.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:12.190
And this is going to
give you everything

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.100
from changes in traffic
day-to-day, week over week.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.710
You can break this down
in different time periods,

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:21.240
get information about change
management and governance data,

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.950
certificate information,
policy configuration data

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:27.580
on a per-API, per-proxy
basis, information

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.460
about your policies.

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.657
So there is more
than 50 elements

00:12:30.657 --> 00:12:32.990
that are going to be included
as part of this dashboard.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:35.200
And this is now
available in Alpha

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.420
today, and will be
available more broadly

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:38.900
later this quarter.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.320
So if we look back at
all the recent data

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.440
breaches I mentioned, if these
organizations had a clear view

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:45.682
into this data--

00:12:45.682 --> 00:12:47.140
security and ops
data-- there would

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:49.663
be many red flags that
would have jumped out long

00:12:49.663 --> 00:12:51.580
before an external
researcher discovered that.

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:55.750
So I'm going to hand things
off to Joel and he's going

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:58.180
to talk more about how API--

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:00.850
how the security of
your infrastructure

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.790
is mission critical
to your APIs.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.340
Thank you.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.020
JOEL D'SA: Thanks, Matt.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.100
And thank you all for coming.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:13.810
It's really early and this
is a wonderful conference.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.090
I hope you're having fun.

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:19.660
So many great sessions and
we're really glad that you chose

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.530
to come and hang out with us.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.000
We hope you'll find
this content useful.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:27.400
Just by way of introduction,
my name is Joel D'sa.

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:29.920
I've been working for
the last 12-plus years

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:33.050
on enterprise security
problems, and APIs

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:36.590
are a key part of your
enterprises' IT infrastructure.

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.890
And I thought I'd spend
some time providing

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:42.490
a perspective of where
API security is today

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:46.265
and, perhaps, provide
some guidance for where

00:13:46.265 --> 00:13:47.140
the future is headed.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:53.240
Now, security is a
very broad field.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.490
There are lots of
things to consider,

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:57.680
many things to think about.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:59.630
And you could try to
wrestle with all of them

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:03.870
at the same time, but
it's pretty difficult.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:05.810
So what we have
found is that if you

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:10.610
use data and analytics to
understand the threats that

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:14.460
are unique to your APIs
and to your environment,

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.400
this will give you
more control and you

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:20.030
will be in a better
position to tailor responses

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:20.870
to those threats.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:29.060
And once you have all the
data and metrics laid out,

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:32.270
you will find that
APIs face threats

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:36.140
that are very similar to
typical software applications,

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:37.730
as well as web apps.

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:41.030
So you will find everything
from basic software issues.

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:43.280
Things like
authorization problems

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:46.940
that were faced by
Equifax and USPS,

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:48.650
or maybe something
like a random number

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:50.067
generator in your
environment that

00:14:50.067 --> 00:14:54.950
is not really random, or perhaps
improper password storage.

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:57.680
This is something that
LinkedIn faced in the past,

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.701
and more recently, Facebook.

00:15:00.701 --> 00:15:04.070
In addition, just like
traditional web applications,

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.440
APIs are also subject
to injection threats

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:09.950
like cross-site scripting,
SQL injection, remote code

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:14.040
execution, and a
whole host of others.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.095
Then there are service
availability issues.

00:15:16.095 --> 00:15:18.390
These are denial
of service issues.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.660
These can be caused by malicious
actors or just some new release

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.060
of an app that's
spiking your traffic.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:25.950
But it could cause a
service availability

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.160
problem for your APIs.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:33.690
And then there's a growing
risk from insiders.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:38.670
A ton of banks, technology
companies, payment gateways,

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:43.350
insurance providers have been
compromised by insider threats,

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:47.040
and so we'll talk a little
bit about them today.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.660
And last, but not
least, spam and abuse.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.930
Now, if you're following
social networks recently

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:57.480
and the security issues
that they're facing,

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:59.160
they're facing a ton
of spam and abuse,

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:02.542
and you will see the same
thing with APIs today.

00:16:02.542 --> 00:16:03.750
And we'll cover some of that.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.840
Now, the first thing
you want to get right

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:11.000
is your basic
software architecture.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.100
And this is something
no one wants

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.690
to address because
it's difficult,

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.720
but it's actually very important
and key to your enterprise

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.280
security.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:25.250
So things like good code, code
reviews, having good tests,

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:28.370
this will allow you to
reproduce security issues

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:31.520
in your environment,
fix them, and roll them

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.040
into production quickly.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.650
Then we found security
reviews to be helpful.

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:40.580
Just having a review ensures
that security defects found

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:44.180
in one part of your API program
don't surface in another part

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:47.240
your API program.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:48.940
And now a lot of
developers today--

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:51.950
unlike a few years earlier,
you just had Java and C Sharp.

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.680
Today, there's a whole host of
software development stacks.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:58.513
You can do use Node and
Rails and Go and Rust.

00:16:58.513 --> 00:16:59.930
And you should use
the tool that's

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.270
best suited for your
problem, but you

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.369
must go in with your eyes open.

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:08.060
Every stack has its own
unique security challenges,

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.130
its own third-party
vulnerability

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:13.079
management process, the
dependencies differ.

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:15.349
And so you must go in
with your eyes open.

00:17:15.349 --> 00:17:18.650
Be prepared to address
the security threats

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:22.661
that your stack poses to you,
and be aware that they exist,

00:17:22.661 --> 00:17:24.869
and be prepared to roll them
into production quickly.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:33.240
Now, a lot of security defects
are found by well-meaning users

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:37.170
and customers of your APIs,
so have a place for them

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.470
to reach out to you, to send
you an email about the problem

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.640
they're seeing.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.240
Build a process
to ensure that you

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:47.790
are able to triage these
issues, fix them, roll them

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.490
into production, and then get
back to the original person who

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:52.740
filed it so that they
can try it out again

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:56.880
and make sure that the
problem is truly fixed.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:59.640
Also, engaging a
third-party penetration test

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.620
is actually very
useful, and that

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:05.310
will help you illuminate
any blind spots in your API

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:08.160
security posture.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.100
And last, but not least, another
thing that developers hate

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.380
is a change management
process, ensuring

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:16.680
that you have clear visibility
into the changes happening

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.230
into your software
infrastructure.

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:19.570
Who's making them?

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:20.910
Why are they making them?

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:22.447
What is the real change?

00:18:22.447 --> 00:18:23.280
And who approved it?

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:24.655
Is someone even
looking into this

00:18:24.655 --> 00:18:26.560
or is this random
change just happening?

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.040
It's also really important.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.920
All of us have to deal with
legacy software and tools,

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.300
and so what I've found useful--

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:36.030
and you can use that too--
is to draw a threat model,

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:37.980
something like the picture here.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:40.038
You could use a
sophisticated tool to do this

00:18:40.038 --> 00:18:41.580
or you could just
draw a simple block

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:45.060
diagram of your infrastructure,
lay out the different pieces,

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.560
and just draw out the
different dependencies.

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:51.120
And this will allow you to do
an analysis of what exactly

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:53.160
exists in your infrastructure.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:55.080
Where are the real
vulnerabilities?

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:57.480
It allows you to
measure every component

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:00.280
on each of these parameters
and stack rank them

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:04.710
so you can get a handle on
this aspect of your security.

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:08.130
Now, all of this information--
or at least some parts of it--

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:11.580
will be available in
the security dashboard

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:12.640
that Matt talked about.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:15.800
And so you can start from there
and continue to build this out.

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:24.240
One of the most
commonly overlooked,

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:27.000
but easy-to-implement
changes, is actually

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.460
just input validation.

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:34.670
Input validation is one of the
best defenses against injection

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:37.110
attacks, like SQL injection,
cross-site scripting,

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:41.400
remote code execution, and
it's very easy to build.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:44.910
Now, APIs, unlike
traditional web applications,

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:47.660
have an open API specification
or a Swagger spec,

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:51.250
so there's well-defined
input and output.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.740
So it's very easy to
identify all these inputs,

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:58.460
categorize them, and just
deploy uniform input validation.

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:00.680
Now, there are two
things to think about.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:06.970
One is consistency and two is
the use of shared libraries.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.530
I'll just talk about
them with an example.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:11.170
Take a look at this hotel.

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.720
There are all these
rooms and all these rooms

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:16.570
have the same-looking door.

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:20.680
Now, suppose the
doors were different.

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:22.360
Some of them had
these push handles,

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.290
and other handles were
these rotating types,

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.380
and then there was
another handle that you

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:27.422
could open the door with.

00:20:27.422 --> 00:20:29.088
And an attacker walked
in and they would

00:20:29.088 --> 00:20:30.380
look at all these differences.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.100
The first thing they would do
is go and try every single door

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:35.650
to try and find
any inconsistency

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.250
that they could break through.

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:42.190
So having a consistent
input validation and layer

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:44.410
in front of your APIs is
actually very powerful,

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:46.720
and we're seeing this
with our APIs as well.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.900
Apigee hosts enterprise
APIs for a ton of customers,

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:53.770
and the customers that have
inconsistent input validation

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.470
or respond slightly differently
every single time actually get

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:57.220
scammed the most.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:04.330
Now, one of the ways
to achieve consistency

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:06.430
is to deploy shared libraries.

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:09.730
So take a look at this example
in this specification here.

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:14.830
Name, tag, and status
are all strings.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:17.020
So explore the
opportunity to see

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:19.810
if you can use the same regular
expression, and maybe even

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:23.890
the same length just
to achieve consistency.

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:25.360
Use a shared library.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.070
If you use a shared library--

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:29.350
sometimes regular expressions
are hard to write.

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:32.990
If you fix it in one place, your
entire platform will benefit.

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:35.350
So it is something you
should at least check out.

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:36.910
Try it out.

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:40.300
And we've used it successfully.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.400
API management
vendors like Apigee

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:46.930
provide a whole host of policies
that allow you to easily deploy

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:49.190
input validation.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:54.400
Now, if someone is scanning your
API and these policies trip,

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:57.280
all that information will show
up on the security dashboard,

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.290
so you can get a
clear understanding

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:01.840
of who is attacking your API.

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:05.290
Working mobile apps and
well-meaning partners

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:08.240
don't trip your input
validation policy.

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:11.020
It's the rest of the people
trying to actually break in.

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:13.270
So you'll get a better
idea of what's going on.

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:20.270
Now, one of the most
common questions

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:22.910
that we get from
customers is, how do you

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:28.070
deal with service availability
issues or DDoS-type situations?

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:29.540
These are very easy to create.

00:22:29.540 --> 00:22:30.560
APIs are out there.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:32.030
The specification is known.

00:22:32.030 --> 00:22:36.350
So anybody can try to
maybe cause a brownout.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:39.920
It could also be caused by buggy
clients or maybe partners that

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.630
have misconfigured
their applications

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:44.240
and are causing
availability threats.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:48.590
And the best way to defend
against API availability

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:54.240
issues and DDoS-type situations
is to actually scale out.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:56.150
And the reason
that is the case is

00:22:56.150 --> 00:23:00.380
that if your infrastructure
is able to successfully defend

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:03.050
against a
denial-of-service attack,

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:06.770
all the attacker needs to
do is ramp up the traffic,

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:08.910
so you need to scale out.

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:11.450
Now, this problem is
not new for Google.

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:13.310
Google has been defending
its infrastructure

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:16.830
for years against
denial-of-service attacks.

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:18.830
So there are a whole
host of tools that Google

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:21.500
offers that you can use.

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:24.350
If you use Google's
cloud load balancers,

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:27.530
these are the same load
balancers that protect Gmail.

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:29.930
They scale very
effectively and are

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:34.440
able to defend against layer
three and below type attacks.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.580
In addition, you will
find that Google's network

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:40.850
is one seamless global network.

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:43.040
It scales to handle
all of Google's traffic

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:45.180
and can handle yours as well.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:48.140
It is equipped with multiple
layers of load balancers

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.150
that are all
denial-of-service aware.

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:52.310
So if an incoming
denial-of-service attack

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.580
is sensed, these load
balancers can drop traffic

00:23:55.580 --> 00:23:57.650
before it hits your back end.

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:00.140
And the goal of any successful
denial-of-service attack

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:01.640
is to actually
protect the back end.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:04.010
Make sure that your
API back end does not

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:07.902
have to deal with any
junk or unwanted traffic.

00:24:07.902 --> 00:24:11.780
Now, to deal with API-level
availability issues,

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:14.730
you can deploy some
policies from Apigee.

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:18.260
Apigee's API management provides
policies like API rate limits.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:23.150
This will allow you to smooth
any spikes in your traffic.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:26.930
In addition, if you deploy
Apigee's globally distributed

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:30.830
and encrypted caches, these
will help save your API back end

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:34.910
by serving up responses
to frequent requests.

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:38.360
And in addition, if you
use resource quotas,

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:40.160
this will allow you
to deploy quotas

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:43.740
on an app or client
or partner base.

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:46.220
And so you can, on a
resource-by-resource level,

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:52.710
defend your APIs
against too much use.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:54.900
Now, the next thing
I wanted to point out

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:57.960
is that any of these
threats that face your APIs

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:01.230
will show up in your
security dashboard,

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:02.820
so you can get a
better understanding

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.970
of which APIs are subject
to such kinds of threats.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.660
But one of the most
important things

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:18.570
that you could do to defend your
APIs against security threats

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:22.860
is actually to protect
the user of those APIs.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:25.080
And sometimes, this
is a hard thing to do

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:27.850
and many people get it wrong.

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:30.660
And I was hoping to spend
some time on this topic,

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:33.630
starting with a history,
because the space has

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:35.520
undergone a series of changes.

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.520
I thought I'd take you through
a history of those changes,

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:41.575
give you a perspective of
where I was coming from,

00:25:41.575 --> 00:25:44.200
and then provide some direction
for where the future is headed.

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.370
So let's start
from the mid-1990s.

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:51.830
Most websites, in the
beginning of the internet,

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:53.300
were really simple.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:55.205
And even today, most
government websites

00:25:55.205 --> 00:25:57.080
that just provide
information-- if any of you

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:59.570
filed your taxes recently
and went to the IRS website,

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:01.290
you'll find something like this.

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:03.500
It's a rather
monolithic website.

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.930
Most of the content is served
from a content management

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:09.230
system or a simple database,
and all of its infrastructure

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:13.790
and code is protected
using a network perimeter.

00:26:13.790 --> 00:26:15.830
So it's very simple.

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:18.440
But as the internet
became more complicated

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:20.390
and websites started
getting more complex,

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:24.590
multiple websites were
deployed to the same container.

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:27.200
And data tiers were
deployed to these containers

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:31.850
that allowed these websites
to accept user input.

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:33.770
And the first user
input, obviously,

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:35.450
was user credentials.

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:39.890
Usernames and passwords
were exchanged for cookies.

00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:41.990
And again, an
infrastructure like this

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:47.360
was protected using
a network perimeter.

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:50.090
Now, as these got
even more complicated,

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:52.490
reusable data components
started getting

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:55.610
deployed to the same container.

00:26:55.610 --> 00:26:58.340
And in order for this
infrastructure to scale,

00:26:58.340 --> 00:27:01.670
multiple ports of the
same kind of layout

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:04.710
were deployed on
a worldwide basis.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:06.830
And there was a concept
of sticky sessions.

00:27:06.830 --> 00:27:09.290
They're still available today.

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:13.310
Sticky sessions ensure
that once you authenticate

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:15.800
to a single part of
the infrastructure,

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:19.070
load balancers will make sure
that all subsequent traffic

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:22.403
from your browser will hit that
same port of infrastructure.

00:27:25.790 --> 00:27:28.000
And then, those of you who
have worked on WebSphere

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:32.120
and WebLogic will recognize
this, where session EJBs were

00:27:32.120 --> 00:27:35.950
deployed to application servers,
and that allowed the data tier

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:38.070
to scale independently
of the web tier.

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:46.830
Now, mobile apps and
stateless UIs using Ajax--

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:48.600
I don't know if
you remember that.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:53.130
That is when things started
to shift a little bit.

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:57.360
So if you went to an airline
website in the mid-2000s

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.030
and they were rolling
out a mobile interface,

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.440
you would see this shift.

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:03.030
When you went to
book a flight, you

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:05.340
would see this wonderful,
fluid experience.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:07.860
You would see all the
prices laid out dynamically

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:08.490
in a table.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:10.020
You could select
the dates and you'd

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:12.340
book a flight and
everything was great.

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:14.130
And then you went to
check flight status

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:15.810
and that was another experience.

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:18.630
You'd try to hit Next,
and it was rather painful.

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:21.760
And this was the transition
everyone was making.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:26.180
But one aspect was the
jarring customer experience.

00:28:26.180 --> 00:28:28.500
What was even more jarring
was the authentication

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:30.720
and authorization
infrastructure deployed

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:32.520
to support such
a kind of an app.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:36.300
So modern stateless UIs,
including mobile apps,

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:41.160
use API gateways, APIs,
tokens, whereas traditional web

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:44.608
applications continue
to use cookies.

00:28:44.608 --> 00:28:46.650
And this difference is
what I wanted to call out.

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:53.240
Now, what you'll notice is
throughout this evolution,

00:28:53.240 --> 00:28:57.830
the underlying security
model hadn't really changed.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:04.400
All the data and logic required
to make security decisions

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:07.850
was constrained to that
same network perimeter.

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:13.550
In many cases, it never
even left the JVM.

00:29:13.550 --> 00:29:15.800
And this is starting to shift.

00:29:15.800 --> 00:29:18.700
This sort of an architecture
no longer makes sense.

00:29:22.910 --> 00:29:24.890
There was a reliance on
that network perimeter,

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:26.750
and perimeters are good.

00:29:26.750 --> 00:29:27.980
They give you peace of mind.

00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:30.320
If you look at this castle
here, it looks great.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:31.778
I would stay in a
castle like that.

00:29:31.778 --> 00:29:32.870
You'd be very secure.

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:35.390
It gives you total
peace of mind.

00:29:35.390 --> 00:29:37.820
It enforces consistency.

00:29:37.820 --> 00:29:40.460
It limits the
damage of a breach.

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:43.190
If that castle was to blow
up, none of the town around it

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:45.930
would ever even feel it.

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:48.140
And it limits the
number of attacks

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:50.072
because it looks secure.

00:29:50.072 --> 00:29:51.530
So there's nothing
wrong with this,

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:57.280
but there are a few things you
want to notice about this now.

00:29:57.280 --> 00:30:04.330
50% of our customers are
moving to using microservices.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:06.940
One thing you want to
notice about microservices

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:10.240
is that, in traditional
web applications,

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:15.540
the applications were deployed
to application servers.

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:18.040
But with microservices-- and
especially if you use something

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:19.120
like Spring Boot--

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:21.740
it's the server that's
deployed to the application.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:24.640
So it's actually the
other way around.

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:26.590
So whereas, in an earlier
architecture, if you

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:29.410
used an application server,
the application server

00:30:29.410 --> 00:30:33.100
handled authentication,
authorization, session

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:38.110
management, with microservices,
there is a need for consistency

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:40.445
because it is now the
role of each microservice

00:30:40.445 --> 00:30:41.320
to take on that load.

00:30:45.040 --> 00:30:48.970
And if you have thousands
of microservices

00:30:48.970 --> 00:30:52.030
all deployed to
Kubernetes clusters

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:55.090
on a worldwide basis
to multiple clouds,

00:30:55.090 --> 00:30:57.592
how do you even
draw a perimeter?

00:30:57.592 --> 00:30:59.751
And this is a really
big challenge.

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:06.740
So what you want to do
is to use an API gateway

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:11.150
to limit the aperture
of what you are exposing

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:14.990
and pair it with an
identity management solution

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:17.450
to use identity to
manage the relationships

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:21.560
between internal service
producers and external service

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:22.160
consumers.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:28.830
You might also choose to
deploy a service mesh that

00:31:28.830 --> 00:31:32.160
will allow you to
compose applications

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:34.530
off your different
microservices.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:37.020
And as I said earlier,
deploy an API gateway

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:39.160
to expose only what's required.

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:47.260
You also want to pair this
with a highly scalable, highly

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:50.660
secure identity
management solution.

00:31:50.660 --> 00:31:53.470
Now, if you choose to use
Google's identity management

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.440
platform, this will
allow you to deploy

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:59.110
a standard consistent
authentication

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:01.390
and authorization layer.

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:06.130
It will use standards-based
frameworks like OAuth 2,

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:08.950
and it will also
prevent fraud and abuse

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:11.140
because this is the
same infrastructure that

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:13.050
defends Google.

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:15.580
The systems will
detect users that

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:18.400
are malicious, that
are trying to break in,

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:22.060
that are trying to do credential
stuffing by reusing passwords,

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:25.400
and will defend against
a whole host of attacks.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:28.660
In addition-- and you should
notice the trend now--

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:31.990
the metrics generated
by this will tell you

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:34.350
who is attacking your
platform and what

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:36.980
are your users
actually trying to do.

00:32:36.980 --> 00:32:40.000
Are they legitimate or not?

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:42.250
So this is something we
highly recommend everybody do.

00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:48.960
Now, let me talk
about another remnant

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:51.120
of the old cookie-based design.

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:53.250
So it was a design
that looked like this.

00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:57.570
A client would typically
take user credentials

00:32:57.570 --> 00:32:59.370
and forward them
on to a service.

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:01.110
The service would
then forward them

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:04.630
on to an authentication server
to check if the user was valid.

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:06.630
It would then check with
an authorization server

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:10.230
to ensure that the user really
had the right level of access.

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:14.430
It's a very common design
pattern, nothing wrong with it.

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:17.260
But as these services
start to grow,

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.530
you will see increasing
load on the authentication

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:21.480
and authorization servers.

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:24.270
Even the slightest
latency at that layer

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:26.550
will present a huge
latency problem

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:29.220
for your entire infrastructure.

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:33.030
In addition, they're
single points of failure.

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:36.620
And one breach,
say, in service one,

00:33:36.620 --> 00:33:38.940
will actually compromise
your entire infrastructure

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:41.550
because the system
does not allow

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:46.020
for time-bound,
delegated access to data.

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:48.210
So service one has access
to all the credentials.

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:50.850
If that is compromised
and that service is hosed,

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:53.640
the entire
infrastructure is gone.

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:56.580
So what we would recommend is
that everyone move to something

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:58.870
that looks like this.

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:05.080
It's very similar, but there
is more onus put on the client.

00:34:05.080 --> 00:34:08.460
So your client will now
exchange credentials

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:13.170
for an OpenID Connect identity
token with an OpenID Connect

00:34:13.170 --> 00:34:15.030
server.

00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:19.170
So the OpenID Connect server
will authenticate the user,

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:21.540
then it will exchange
the identity token

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:22.949
for an access token.

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:25.909
And the access token, if you
use something like OAuth 2,

00:34:25.909 --> 00:34:29.550
will give you time-bound,
delegated access only

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:31.560
to service one.

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:33.980
Now, if you use a JAR token--

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.630
JAR tokens are very
common nowadays,

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:39.120
but they're basically
just [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:42.060
encoded blobs of data that
are assigned by the server.

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:45.840
So all the service needs to
do is just open up the token

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.340
and validate the signature.

00:34:47.340 --> 00:34:50.489
And now, in every request,
you have all the information

00:34:50.489 --> 00:34:52.500
required, from a
security perspective,

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:54.000
to complete the request.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:57.120
So the service does not have to
make any outbound calls to do

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:58.350
anything.

00:34:58.350 --> 00:35:01.920
So JAR tokens will help
your service scale better.

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:04.620
OAuth 2 will give you a
consistent authentication

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:08.280
and authorization experience,
and will provide you

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:11.160
a framework to implement
time-bound, delegated access

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:15.720
to data that will
limit the breaches.

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:21.900
And then, from a traditional
architecture point of view,

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:24.300
this is what is
called Zero Trust.

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:26.550
Authenticate everyone,
every time, all the time.

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:30.690
So that's what you might
hear commonly as Zero Trust.

00:35:30.690 --> 00:35:33.240
So it is a way forward
to implement a Zero Trust

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:34.204
architecture.

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.470
Now, one of the things
a lot of security folks

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:42.890
have to pay attention to
is the user experience

00:35:42.890 --> 00:35:44.780
because that's where
everything will break.

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:46.940
So if you deploy this
wonderful security

00:35:46.940 --> 00:35:49.580
and you mess up the
user experience,

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:51.840
that's where you'll
see a breach.

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:54.050
So this is an example.

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:55.850
We're working with
a retail customer.

00:35:55.850 --> 00:35:59.840
So retailers have a
whole host of partners

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:03.150
that they work with
to be successful.

00:36:03.150 --> 00:36:06.110
And in this particular case,
the partner reached out

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:09.350
to the API team and said, hey,
I want to access this API.

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:11.130
And the API team
was super excited,

00:36:11.130 --> 00:36:12.800
and so they contacted
the security team

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:14.740
to get a key issued.

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:17.580
And now the security team does
not have any context on this,

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:20.420
so they're trying to figure
out the right scope of access.

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:22.610
So they tried to
create the right rules,

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:25.520
and they made the key,
and they hope it's right.

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:27.530
And now they have to
send the key over.

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:30.470
So they would encrypt
it using their PGP key,

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:32.940
and then send it to a manager
who would do the same thing.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:35.600
And they would transfer that
to the app developer, who

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:38.210
would then use an
out-of-bound channel,

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:40.490
maybe call them up and
figure out what the key was.

00:36:40.490 --> 00:36:43.440
And then, finally, it would
work until it was time

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:44.690
to change the scope of access.

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:47.380
And then the entire process
would have to be re-roled,

00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:49.850
or they had to unload the
partner, revoke their access,

00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:52.010
and then, again,
there was a mess.

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:54.380
So a lot of our
customers use what's

00:36:54.380 --> 00:36:56.240
called a developer portal.

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:59.510
A developer border
gives API consumers

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:03.290
a self-service experience so
that they can go and check out

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:07.010
the APIs, understand what
is available for access

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:12.140
and what is not so that they
don't request the wrong access.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:13.543
In this case,
Mercedes-Benz-- you

00:37:13.543 --> 00:37:15.210
should check out their
developer portal.

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:16.730
They've done a fantastic job.

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:19.670
So the developer portal
explains all the APIs

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:21.620
that are available,
what they can do,

00:37:21.620 --> 00:37:23.690
allows the developer
to try it out,

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:27.410
and then allows the user
to register for a key.

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:29.570
And you can then build
an approval workflow

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:33.530
on the back end that will
allow the right people to look

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:36.530
at the request, make
sure it's valid,

00:37:36.530 --> 00:37:40.730
and have a key automated
securely delivered

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:44.150
to the API team.

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:46.730
Now, again, one more thing
you want to call out here

00:37:46.730 --> 00:37:50.510
is that all the information
from this developer portal

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:54.250
will be made available in
the security dashboard.

00:37:54.250 --> 00:37:59.060
And so you can get a better idea
of who is accessing your API.

00:37:59.060 --> 00:38:01.580
Is it valid or invalid?

00:38:01.580 --> 00:38:02.843
Is it malicious or not?

00:38:02.843 --> 00:38:04.760
Is someone poking around
your developer portal

00:38:04.760 --> 00:38:07.100
just for the fun of it?

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:10.610
Has that same user breached
another part of your website?

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:13.610
So it's actually a great
way to get awareness

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:15.470
into what's happening
with your API program.

00:38:20.620 --> 00:38:21.340
Great.

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:24.970
Can I have a show of hands for
how many of you are concerned

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:27.980
about insider threats?

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:28.810
Wow.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:30.400
We did the session
on Tuesday and this

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:34.780
is a much bigger audience and
a much better show of hands.

00:38:34.780 --> 00:38:37.870
Insider threats are actually
a commonly overlooked problem,

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:41.060
but a huge problem for
a lot of enterprises.

00:38:41.060 --> 00:38:43.300
There are a ton of
privileged users

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:49.150
that have access to lots of data
and maybe they shouldn't have.

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:51.580
If they do, maybe they
shouldn't use it unilaterally.

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:56.000
They should have someone
else provide some approval.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:57.880
And they're an
increasing problem

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:02.110
for enterprise security.

00:39:02.110 --> 00:39:04.810
Time-bound, delegated
access does not only

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:08.110
apply to API authentication.

00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:11.500
It also applies
to your employees

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:13.900
and their contractors,
and anyone with access

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:16.968
to your API program.

00:39:16.968 --> 00:39:18.760
So what you want to do
is-- the first thing

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:21.370
is integrate your API
management solution,

00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:25.150
as well as your Google Cloud
Platform with your enterprise's

00:39:25.150 --> 00:39:26.940
single sign on infrastructure.

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:28.930
And that's a great first start.

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.780
It allows you to
provide a consistent way

00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:35.920
of looking at who is doing
what in your infrastructure.

00:39:35.920 --> 00:39:37.143
What are they trying to do?

00:39:37.143 --> 00:39:38.560
Are they doing
something malicious

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:41.080
or are they poking around
where they shouldn't be?

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:42.230
Things of that nature.

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.770
And then the same things
you use to protect your web

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:46.617
mail, like two-factor
authentication

00:39:46.617 --> 00:39:48.700
or what have you-- risk-based
authentication-- you

00:39:48.700 --> 00:39:52.930
can use the same way to
protect your API platform.

00:39:52.930 --> 00:39:54.760
Now, the second
thing you want to do

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:57.130
is to deploy change management.

00:39:57.130 --> 00:39:59.860
That is, to understand
who is making

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:01.690
what changes to your APIs.

00:40:01.690 --> 00:40:02.930
What are the changes?

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:04.030
Why are they being made?

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:05.680
Is there some sort of a design?

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:07.450
Is there some sort
of an approval?

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:12.190
So there's some governance
and visibility into what's

00:40:12.190 --> 00:40:15.160
happening to your API program.

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:18.310
And again, we hope to provide
some of this information

00:40:18.310 --> 00:40:19.810
in the security dashboard.

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:22.150
It will give you a good view
of the changes happening

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:24.040
to your APIs so you
can get a better

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:26.420
idea-- when you integrate
it with your other tools,

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:28.825
you will get a holistic view
of the changes happening

00:40:28.825 --> 00:40:30.790
to your infrastructure
and whether there

00:40:30.790 --> 00:40:32.200
is some visibility into it.

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:38.990
And last, but not
least, I wanted

00:40:38.990 --> 00:40:41.480
to talk about spam and abuse.

00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:44.780
Now, again, we host a
ton of enterprise APIs.

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:47.420
Many of our retailers
are very, very concerned

00:40:47.420 --> 00:40:48.770
about Black Friday.

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:52.140
And we see a lot
of spam and abuse,

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:55.190
especially on
high-traffic events.

00:40:55.190 --> 00:40:59.480
And this is everything from
credential stuffing, people

00:40:59.480 --> 00:41:03.320
just trying to brute force the
API, or just content scraping.

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:06.783
For a retailer, price
matching is a very big deal,

00:41:06.783 --> 00:41:08.450
so you might just
want to content scrape

00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:10.250
to figure out what's going on.

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:13.790
And so there are a whole
host of automated actors

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:17.210
who are trying to
break open the API.

00:41:17.210 --> 00:41:20.300
There are automated bots
that are constantly scanning

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:22.560
these APIs for any changes.

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:25.670
And obviously, abuse and
bots and denial-of-service

00:41:25.670 --> 00:41:28.280
are not new problems
to Google, and we

00:41:28.280 --> 00:41:32.760
are bringing the best of Google
to help you deal with this.

00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:38.870
So there are two broad solutions
to spam and abuse defense.

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:43.700
One is a prevention system
that you can deploy,

00:41:43.700 --> 00:41:46.250
and the other is a
visibility system

00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:49.970
that will allow you to get
better visibility into APIs.

00:41:49.970 --> 00:41:52.050
So from a prevention
point of view,

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:57.050
your goal is to put
maximum onus on the client

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.982
to prove who they say they are.

00:41:59.982 --> 00:42:01.940
One of the best ways,
again, is to authenticate

00:42:01.940 --> 00:42:03.840
the users and the clients.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:05.840
But we recognize
that a lot of APIs

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:08.340
today don't need to
be authenticated,

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:11.930
or maybe authentication
is too high of a barrier.

00:42:11.930 --> 00:42:16.033
So what you can do is deploy
a system like reCAPTCHA.

00:42:16.033 --> 00:42:17.450
ReCAPTCHA-- I don't
know if you've

00:42:17.450 --> 00:42:18.620
noticed it on many websites.

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:20.090
It's a simple box
with a checkbox

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:22.670
and then it shows
up a challenge.

00:42:22.670 --> 00:42:26.340
And the challenge then
slows down the user.

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:28.710
They have to actually
do something.

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:32.197
And so it slows down the
rate of spam and abuse

00:42:32.197 --> 00:42:34.280
because automated bots
can't solve that challenge,

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:35.930
in many cases.

00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:38.810
But last year, if you don't want
that kind of a disruptive user

00:42:38.810 --> 00:42:41.940
experience-- pick out
a goat from this map--

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:43.760
you might want to
deploy version 3, which

00:42:43.760 --> 00:42:45.110
was launched last year.

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:47.150
You should definitely
check it out.

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:50.990
Version 3 gives you
a reputation score,

00:42:50.990 --> 00:42:54.950
and that will allow you to
make a ton of access decisions

00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:57.110
based on their reputation score.

00:42:57.110 --> 00:42:59.353
And yesterday, that
same team launched

00:42:59.353 --> 00:43:01.520
what's called reCAPTCHA
Enterprise with a whole host

00:43:01.520 --> 00:43:03.140
of enterprise-ready features.

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:05.720
And this, if you deploy, will
give you a much better idea

00:43:05.720 --> 00:43:09.650
into spam and abuse and allow
you to take control of it.

00:43:09.650 --> 00:43:12.980
Now, the second thing you
want to do is gain visibility.

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:15.530
Google has a product
called Cloud Armor.

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:17.600
If you deploy this in
front of your APIs,

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:20.540
you will get a better view
into the metadata surrounding

00:43:20.540 --> 00:43:22.010
your API accesses.

00:43:22.010 --> 00:43:23.750
What are the IP addresses?

00:43:23.750 --> 00:43:24.900
Where are they coming from?

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:25.700
What locations?

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:26.772
Can they be blocked?

00:43:26.772 --> 00:43:27.980
Do they have other histories?

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:30.830
So you'll get a better
idea of what is actually

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:32.540
hitting your API endpoints.

00:43:32.540 --> 00:43:34.250
In addition, Apigee
has a product

00:43:34.250 --> 00:43:38.060
called Apigee Sense that
does analytics on your APIs

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:41.142
that will allow you to tell
whether this is a credential

00:43:41.142 --> 00:43:43.100
stuffing attack, someone
trying to brute force,

00:43:43.100 --> 00:43:44.960
or content scrape,
and will classify

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:46.325
the different kinds of attacks.

00:43:46.325 --> 00:43:48.200
You can get a better
idea of what's going on.

00:43:51.160 --> 00:43:53.430
So we covered a lot
of content today.

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:56.000
I hope it was useful.

00:43:56.000 --> 00:43:58.290
So I hope it is also
clear that using

00:43:58.290 --> 00:44:01.650
a data and
metrics-driven approach

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:07.440
is actually mission critical
to improving your API security.

00:44:07.440 --> 00:44:10.200
And in addition, with
the ever-expanding world

00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:15.390
of microservices,
APIs, mobile apps,

00:44:15.390 --> 00:44:18.660
your architecture really
needs to change in order

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:23.340
to improve your security and
keep your users and data safe.

00:44:23.340 --> 00:44:24.390
Thank you very much.

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:26.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:30.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

